  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Hot, dry, and smoky conditions continue through this week. \n\n- Isolated thunderstorms remain a threat for southwest Montana\n  including Butte. Potentially expanding northward late this week.\n\n- Even hotter, drier, and windier conditions by Saturday. \n  Elevated fire weather conditions. \n\n- Cool down to near average temperatures Sunday, though it \n  remains dry.\n  \n\nThe Northern Rockies will remain along the western periphery of a\nbroad ridge of high pressure. Toward the end of the work week, \nthe ridge will amplify as a deep, stationary Pacific trough moves \neast into British Columbia. Beginning as early as Friday, embedded\nshortwave energy ejecting off the trough could interact with the \nridge. Current guidance targets late Saturday into early Sunday \nmorning for the ridge breakdown.\n\nHot, dry, and smoky conditions will be common this week. \nDepending on the thickness of wildfire smoke, daytime temperatures\ncould be attenuated several degrees from the current forecast. \nDegraded air quality and reduced visibility remain the primary \nimpacts. Sensitive groups should limit outdoor exertion.\n\nThe only hint at precipitation for the first part of the week is \nisolated thunderstorm chances in Lemhi County and southwest \nMontana (mainly afternoons/evenings) as limited monsoonal moisture\ninteracts with daytime heating. Beginning Wednesday and lasting \nthrough Friday, models show potential disturbances that could \nexpand thunderstorm coverage further north into west-central \nMontana and north-central Idaho. If thunderstorms do develop, they\nwould be mostly dry with gusty outflow potential. There is a \nconcern for nocturnal storms, particularly on Thursday night but \noverall confidence is low at this point. \n\nThere is a growing consensus that a ridge breakdown will occur \nlater Saturday into Sunday based on the latest ensemble clusters. \nThis transition period is significant given increased wind \npotential and elevated fire weather concerns.",
